<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # How Are You? – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I'm Happy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Are You?</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication: Relay messages from teacher to peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use gestures to relay the meaning of emotion-related language to peers (e.g., smiling to show 'I'm happy.'). Can use gestures and miming to relay information about basic needs (e.g., rubbing the stomach to show 'I'm hungry.'). Can interpret and convey simple 'I'm...' statements using body language (e.g., pretending to sleep to indicate 'I'm tired.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 45%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with the "How Are You?" song (e.g., "Hello, hello, how are you? I'm happy, I'm happy, how are you?"). Students sing along and mimic actions for 'happy,' 'sad,' and 'sleepy.' Teacher uses flashcards of 'happy,' 'sad,' and 'tired' and asks students to show with their faces and bodies how they feel. This activates prior knowledge about feelings and introduces non-verbal communication.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models asking "How are you?" and responding "I'm happy!" while smiling and making a thumbs-up gesture. Then, the teacher models "I'm tired!" with a yawn and rubbing eyes, demonstrating how to relay a message using body language. The teacher clearly pronounces /h/ in 'happy' and /e/ in 'excited' as new vocabulary such as 'excited' will be introduced. The teacher also shows how to point to a 'friend' and ask the question.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher smiles broadly and points to self) "Am I happy?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher yawns and rubs eyes) "Is this 'happy' or 'tired'?" (Expected answer: "Tired!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful picture flashcards depicting various feelings (happy, sad, tired, hungry, thirsty, excited, angry). Each card has a clear image and the word written below. The teacher also introduces simple sentences 'I'm happy.', 'I'm sad.', 'I'm tired.' related to the images.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Discussing feelings: Talking about emotions and recognizing others' feelings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Show Me the Feeling":</b> The teacher shows a flashcard (e.g., 'hungry') and asks, "How is he/she feeling?" Students point to their stomachs or rub them to relay the meaning. The teacher then says "I'm hungry." The teacher continues with other cards (e.g., 'thirsty' - pointing to mouth, 'excited' - jumping, 'calm' - deep breath).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Match the Mimic":</b> The teacher mimes a feeling (e.g., pretending to sleep for 'I'm tired.') and asks, "Which picture is this?" Students point to the correct flashcard or mimic the action back. This reinforces recognizing letters of the alphabet in familiar words like 'E' in 'rest' or 'H' in 'happy' on the flashcards.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Pragmatic: Expressing feelings and needs by saying, 'I'm happy.' or 'I'm tired.' Responding to 'How are you?' with simple 'I'm...' statements. Grammar: Simple sentences ('I'm happy.'), I'm + adjective ('I'm excited.'). Vocabulary: happy, sad, tired, hungry, thirsty, excited.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - "Teacher Says, You Show":</b> The teacher says a feeling (e.g., "I'm sad!"). Students must use gestures or mime to relay the meaning to their neighbor. The teacher observes if students correctly interpret the spoken word and relay it non-verbally. This includes actions for 'smile,' 'frown,' 'hug,' and 'help' if applicable to the feeling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled - "Feelings Message Chain":</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher whispers a feeling (e.g., "I'm hungry.") to the first student. That student must *mime* the feeling to the next student in the circle, silently relaying the message. The next student then mimes it to the next, and so on. The last student in the chain says the feeling aloud. This activity focuses on interpreting and conveying messages non-verbally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - "Ask and Show":</b> In pairs, one student asks, "How are you?" The other student chooses a feeling (e.g., 'happy,' 'sleepy,' 'sick') and responds using gestures/miming (e.g., smiling, pretending to sleep, rubbing forehead). The first student tries to guess the feeling. They can also point to a flashcard with the feeling. This encourages active relaying and interpretation between peers.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative game focusing on relaying information for the "Feelings Bingo" project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description: "Bingo Feelings Relay"</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains that the students will create a "Feelings Bingo" game. For today, they will practice the *relay* part of the game.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a simplified "Bingo" card (a small card with 4-6 pre-printed pictures of different feelings: happy, sad, tired, hungry, excited, angry).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher whispers a feeling (e.g., 'I'm thirsty.') to one student in each group. This student acts as the "relayer."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "relayer" must use gestures and miming (e.g., rubbing their stomach for 'hungry,' yawning for 'tired,' pointing to their mouth for 'thirsty') to convey this feeling *silently* to their group members.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The group members must interpret the gesture and collaboratively point to the correct feeling on their Bingo card. If they choose correctly, they put a small marker (e.g., a button or a small block) on that picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher rotates and whispers new feelings, encouraging all students to take turns as the "relayer." This activity specifically trains students to relay information about feelings using body language, which is central to the project's 'matching game based on classmates' feelings' aspect.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Bingo Feelings Relay" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Bingo Feelings Relay" game, demonstrating their ability to both relay a given feeling through gesture/miming and interpret a peer's mimed feeling by pointing to the correct picture on their Bingo card.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Relaying messages:</b> Can use gestures and miming to relay the meaning of emotion-related language (e.g., smiling for 'happy,' rubbing stomach for 'hungry,' pretending to sleep for 'tired').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interpreting messages:</b> Can interpret peers' gestures and miming for basic emotions and needs, and correctly identify the corresponding feeling on the Bingo card.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participates actively and cooperates with peers in the group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity:</b> Gestures are clear enough for peers to understand the intended feeling.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher asks) "Show me with your face: Are you 'happy' or 'sad' about today's game?" (Students smile or frown).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher asks) "Show me with your hands: Can you tell a friend how you feel without words?" (Students give a thumbs up or thumbs down gesture).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did we do today with our feelings?" (Expected: "We showed happy/sad/tired," "We played a game.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did we tell our friends how we feel without using many words?" (Expected: "With our bodies," "Miming.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson, especially regarding mediation activities? The "Feelings Message Chain" and "Bingo Feelings Relay" were highly engaging and clearly demonstrated students' ability to relay and interpret non-verbal messages. TPR was effective for Kinder.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled? Some students needed more direct modeling for specific gestures (e.g., 'sick' or 'angry'). Using more exaggerated gestures and providing individual prompts for reluctant participants could be beneficial. Reinforce phonemic awareness for W, X, Y, Z in other contexts.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "How Are You?" and the specific 21st Century Skills Project "Feelings Bingo." The next lesson will introduce a new theme, possibly building on describing oneself or others, and may revisit expressing needs with new vocabulary. The successful completion of the mediation aspect of the Feelings Bingo project provides a strong foundation for future collaborative tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>